**Diary — 2026-02-14 — Porting the CUDA engine to HIP on gfx906**

Today I moved the core CUDA engine onto HIP for a Vega-class GPU, target `gfx906`. The goal was not a full rewrite, just enough to get the existing kernel pipeline running under ROCm with predictable performance.

The first concrete step was to replace CUDA-specific launch syntax with HIP equivalents. The old CUDA code had:

```cpp
__global__ void reduce_kernel(float* out, const float* in, int n) {
    int tid = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
    int stride = blockDim.x * gridDim.x;
    float acc = 0.0f;

    for (int i = tid; i < n; i += stride) {
        acc += in[i];
    }

    __shared__ float smem[256];
    smem[threadIdx.x] = acc;
    __syncthreads();

    for (int s = blockDim.x / 2; s > 0; s >>= 1) {
        if (threadIdx.x < s) {
            smem[threadIdx.x] += smem[threadIdx.x + s];
        }
        __syncthreads();
    }

    if (threadIdx.x == 0) {
        atomicAdd(out, smem[0]);
    }
}
```

Under HIP this mostly compiles as-is, but the launch path changed:

```cpp
reduce_kernel<<<grid, block, 0, stream>>>(out, in, n);
```

became:

```cpp
reduce_kernel<<<grid, block, 0, stream>>>(out, in, n);
```

The syntax is the same, but the runtime calls had to be swapped:

```cpp
cudaMalloc(&d_in, bytes);
cudaMemcpy(d_in, h_in, bytes, cudaMemcpyHostToDevice);
cudaDeviceSynchronize();
```

became:

```cpp
hipMalloc(&d_in, bytes);
hipMemcpy(d_in, h_in, bytes, hipMemcpyHostToDevice);
hipDeviceSynchronize();
```

